<h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section A: Multiple Choice Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Choose the correct answer for each of the following questions.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q1.</strong> A particle of mass m is moving in a circular path of radius r with a uniform speed v. The change in momentum when the particle moves from one end of a diameter to the other end is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 2mv</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) mv</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) &radic;<span style="border-top:1px solid #000;padding-top:1px;margin-left:1px;">2</span>mv</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) zero</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q2.</strong> A body of mass 2 kg is hung on a spring balance mounted in a lift. If the lift is descending with an acceleration of 2  m/s<sup>2</sup>, the reading of the spring balance will be (g = 10  m/s<sup>2</sup>): </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 24 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 20 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 16 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 4 N</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q3.</strong> Three forces acting on a body are kept in equilibrium. Then F<sub>1</sub> = 10  N, F<sub>2</sub> = 6  N. The third force F<sub>3</sub> cannot be: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 12 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 4 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 2 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 8 N</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q4.</strong> A block of mass M is pulled along a horizontal frictionless surface by a rope of mass m. If a force F is applied at the free end of the rope, the force exerted by the rope on the block is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">FM</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">M + m</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">Fm</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">M + m</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">FM</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">m</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) F</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q5.</strong> The coefficient of static friction between a block and a horizontal floor is 0.4. A horizontal force of 20 N is applied to the block of mass 5 kg. The frictional force acting on the block is (g = 10  m/s<sup>2</sup>): </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 20 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 10 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 15 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 25 N</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q6.</strong> A rocket with a lift-off mass 20,000 kg is blasted upwards with an initial acceleration of 5.0  m/s<sup>2</sup>. The initial thrust of the blast is (g = 10  m/s<sup>2</sup>): </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 3 &times; 10<sup>5</sup>  N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 1 &times; 10<sup>5</sup>  N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 2 &times; 10<sup>5</sup>  N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 3 &times; 10<sup>4</sup>  N</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q7.</strong> Which of the following statements is not true about inertial frames of reference? </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Newton's laws are valid in these frames</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) An accelerated frame is an inertial frame</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) A frame moving with uniform velocity is an inertial frame</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) The earth is an approximate inertial frame</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q8.</strong> A string of mass m and length L is hanging from a rigid support. The tension in the string at a distance x from the support is (where M is mass suspended at the bottom, or just self-weight if no mass): if only string's self-weight is considered, tension at distance x from top is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">mg(L-x)</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">L</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">mgx</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">L</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">mg(L+x)</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">L</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) mg</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q9.</strong> A person is standing in an elevator. In which situation he finds his weight less than actual weight? </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) elevator moving up with constant acceleration</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) elevator moving down with constant acceleration</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) elevator moving up with uniform velocity</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) elevator stationary</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q10.</strong> An iron block and a wooden block of the same mass are dropped from the same height in vacuum. Which of them reaches the ground first? </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) iron block</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) wooden block</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) both simultaneously</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) depends on density</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q11.</strong> The maximum velocity of a vehicle on a curved road of radius R and coefficient of friction &mu; is given by: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) &radic;<span style="border-top:1px solid #000;padding-top:1px;margin-left:1px;">&mu; Rg</span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) &mu; Rg</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) &radic;<span style="border-top:1px solid #000;padding-top:1px;margin-left:1px;"><span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">R</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">&mu; g</span></span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">&mu;</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">Rg</span></span></li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q12.</strong> For safe driving on a banked curved road, the angle of banking &theta; is related to velocity v and radius R as: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) tan&theta; = <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">v<sup>2</sup></span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">Rg</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) tan&theta; = <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">Rg</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">v<sup>2</sup></span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) sin&theta; = <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">v<sup>2</sup></span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">Rg</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) cos&theta; = <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">v<sup>2</sup></span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">Rg</span></span></li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q13.</strong> A bullet of mass 10 g is fired from a gun of mass 1 kg with a velocity of 100  m/s. The recoil velocity of the gun is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) -1 m/s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) -0.1 m/s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) -10 m/s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) -0.01 m/s</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q14.</strong> Linear momentum of a body is increased by 50%. The percentage increase in its kinetic energy is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 50%</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 100%</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 125%</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 25%</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q15.</strong> A force F = &alpha; t + &beta; t<sup>2</sup> acts on a particle of mass m. The change in momentum of the particle from t = 0 to t = T is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">&alpha; T<sup>2</sup></span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">2</span></span> + <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">&beta; T<sup>3</sup></span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">3</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) &alpha; T + &beta; T<sup>2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) &alpha; T<sup>2</sup> + &beta; T<sup>3</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">&alpha; T</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">2</span></span> + <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">&beta; T</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">3</span></span></li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q16.</strong> A body of mass 0.5 kg travels in a straight line with velocity v = k x<sup>3/2</sup> where k = 5  m<sup>-1/2</sup>s<sup>-1</sup>. The work done by the force during its displacement from x = 0 to x = 2  m is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 25 J</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 50 J</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 100 J</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 12.5 J</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q17.</strong> Which of the following is a self-adjusting force? </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) static friction</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) rolling friction</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) limiting friction</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) kinetic friction</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q18.</strong> A constant force acts on a body of mass m initially at rest. The relation between distance x travelled and time t is proportional to: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) t<sup>2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) t</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) &radic;<span style="border-top:1px solid #000;padding-top:1px;margin-left:1px;">t</span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) t<sup>3</sup></li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q19.</strong> When a horse pulls a cart, the force that causes the horse to move forward is the force exerted by: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) the ground on the horse</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) the horse on the ground</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) the cart on the horse</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) the horse on the cart</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q20.</strong> An object of mass 3 kg is at rest. A force <b>F&#8407;</b> = (6i&#x0302; + 4j&#x0302;)  N acts on it. The velocity of the object at t = 3  s is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) (6i&#x0302; + 4j&#x0302;)  m/s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) (18i&#x0302; + 12j&#x0302;)  m/s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) (2i&#x0302; + <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">4</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">3</span></span>j&#x0302;)  m/s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) (3i&#x0302; + 2j&#x0302;)  m/s</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q21.</strong> A person driving a car suddenly applies brakes. Passengers are thrown forward. This is due to: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) inertia of motion</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) inertia of rest</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) inertia of direction</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) gravitational pull</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q22.</strong> The minimum force required to start pushing a body up a rough inclined plane of inclination &theta; is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) mg(sin&theta; + &mu;cos&theta;)</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) mg(sin&theta; - &mu;cos&theta;)</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) mg(cos&theta; + &mu;sin&theta;)</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) mgtan&theta;</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q23.</strong> If the tension in the cable of an elevator is equal to the weight of the elevator, the elevator may be: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) moving up with uniform speed</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) at rest</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) moving down with uniform speed</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) all of these</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q24.</strong> A particle moves in the xy-plane under the action of a force F such that the value of its linear momentum at any time t is p<sub>x</sub> = 2cos t, p<sub>y</sub> = 2sin t. The angle between <b>p&#8407;</b> and <b>F&#8407;</b> is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 90°</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 0°</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 180°</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 45°</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q25.</strong> Two masses m<sub>1</sub> and m<sub>2</sub> are connected by a string passing over a frictionless pulley. If m<sub>1</sub> > m<sub>2</sub>, the acceleration of the system is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) &le;ft(<span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">m<sub>1</sub> - m<sub>2</sub></span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub></span></span>\right)g</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) &le;ft(<span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub></span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">m<sub>1</sub> - m<sub>2</sub></span></span>\right)g</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) &le;ft(<span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">m<sub>1</sub></span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub></span></span>\right)g</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) &le;ft(<span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">m<sub>2</sub></span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub></span></span>\right)g</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q26.</strong> The apparent weight of a body in an elevator is zero when: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) lift falls freely under gravity</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) lift moves up with uniform acceleration</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) lift moves down with uniform speed</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) lift is stationary</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q27.</strong> A particle is moving with a constant speed along a circle of radius r. The magnitude of average acceleration for half complete revolution is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">2v<sup>2</sup></span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">&pi; r</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">v<sup>2</sup></span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">r</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">&pi; v<sup>2</sup></span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">r</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) zero</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q28.</strong> A machine gun fires 240 bullets per minute with a velocity of 500  m/s. If the mass of each bullet is 50 g, the power of the gun is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 50 kW</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 100 kW</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 25 kW</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 10 kW</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q29.</strong> The impulse on a particle due to a force F(t) acting from t<sub>1</sub> to t<sub>2</sub> is equal to: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) change in momentum</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) change in kinetic energy</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) rate of change of momentum</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) work done</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q30.</strong> A body of mass M is placed on a rough horizontal surface. The coefficient of friction is &mu;. A force is applied at an angle &theta; to the horizontal. The minimum force required to move the body is when tan&theta; is equal to: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) &mu;</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 1/&mu;</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) &mu;<sup>2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) &radic;<span style="border-top:1px solid #000;padding-top:1px;margin-left:1px;">&mu;</span></li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q31.</strong> Newton's second law of motion gives the measure of: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) force</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) inertia</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) momentum</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) acceleration</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q32.</strong> Action and reaction forces: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) act on the same body</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) act on different bodies</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) have same direction</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) need not be equal in magnitude</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q33.</strong> A coin is placed on a rotating turn-table. If the coefficient of static friction is 0.5 and the radius of the turn-table is 0.5 m, the maximum angular velocity with which the turn-table can rotate without slipping is (g = 10  m/s<sup>2</sup>): </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) &radic;<span style="border-top:1px solid #000;padding-top:1px;margin-left:1px;">10</span>  rad/s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 5  rad/s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 2.5  rad/s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 10  rad/s</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q34.</strong> Which force is always directed towards the center in a circular motion? </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) centripetal force</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) centrifugal force</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) gravitational force</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) frictional force</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q35.</strong> A sphere of mass 1 kg is attached to a string of length 1 m and spun in a vertical circle. The minimum speed at the highest point for the string not to slack is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) &radic;<span style="border-top:1px solid #000;padding-top:1px;margin-left:1px;">g</span>  m/s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) &radic;<span style="border-top:1px solid #000;padding-top:1px;margin-left:1px;">10</span>  m/s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 10  m/s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) zero</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q36.</strong> The dimensional formula of impulse is same as that of: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) linear momentum</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) angular momentum</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) force</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) pressure</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q37.</strong> A block of mass 4 kg is kept on a smooth horizontal surface. Two forces 10 N and 5 N act on it in opposite directions. The acceleration of the block is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 1.25  m/s<sup>2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 3.75  m/s<sup>2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 2.5  m/s<sup>2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 5  m/s<sup>2</sup></li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q38.</strong> If the forces acting on a body are balanced, then the body: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) must be at rest</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) must have uniform velocity</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) either at rest or in uniform motion</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) must be accelerating</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q39.</strong> The angle of repose &lambda; and coefficient of static friction &mu;<sub>s</sub> are related as: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) &mu;<sub>s</sub> = tan&lambda;</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) &mu;<sub>s</sub> = cot&lambda;</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) &mu;<sub>s</sub> = sin&lambda;</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) &mu;<sub>s</sub> = cos&lambda;</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q40.</strong> A man is standing on a weighing machine inside a lift. When the lift goes down with acceleration a (a < g), the reading of the weighing machine is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) m(g - a)</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) m(g + a)</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) mg</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) ma</li></ul><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section B: Fill in the Blanks</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Fill in the blanks with appropriate words or values.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q1.</strong> Newton's first law of motion defines _______. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q2.</strong> The property of a body by virtue of which it opposes any change in its state of rest or uniform motion is called _______. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q3.</strong> The rate of change of momentum of a body is directly proportional to the applied force and takes place in the direction in which the force acts. This is _______ law of motion. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q4.</strong> The SI unit of impulse is _______. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q5.</strong> Friction can be reduced by using _______. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q6.</strong> The maximum value of static friction is called _______ friction. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q7.</strong> Centripetal force is directed towards the _______ of the circular path. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q8.</strong> In a frame of reference that is accelerated, _______ forces must be considered to apply Newton's laws. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q9.</strong> The angle between the direction of force and displacement for maximum work is _______ degrees. </div>
